# x86Sim configuration: copy to x86sim.env (gitignored) and adjust. Plain sh assignments; $X86SIM_ROOT is the
# repo root. Environment variables of the same name override this file. Never put the sudo password here: export
# SUDO_PASS in the shell that needs it (Darling install, killing setuid launchers, cores) or use a cached sudo.

# Darling install prefix (cmake -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=...; bin/darling must be setuid root)
X86SIM_PREFIX=$X86SIM_ROOT/darling-prefix
# RuntimeRoot of the iOS 27.2 (24B8117) arm64 simulator runtime (x86sim runtime fetch 24B8117)
X86SIM_RUNTIME=$X86SIM_ROOT/runtimes/ios-27.2-24B8117-arm64/RuntimeRoot
# Darling build directory (cmake -B; make darling configures it)
#X86SIM_DARLING_BUILD=$X86SIM_ROOT/external/darling/build
# the default phone for CLI commands that take none (x86sim device create / the launcher make others; its data/ is the
# phone's home). Its UDID is derived from this path, so keep it stable once the phone has booted.
X86SIM_DEVICE=$X86SIM_ROOT/var/device-a64
# sockets, pid files and logs of a boot (mtlvk.sock, mDNSResponder, boot.log, ...)
X86SIM_STATE=$X86SIM_ROOT/var/run
# large scratch files: cores, maps (never /tmp if it is a small tmpfs)
X86SIM_SCRATCH=$HOME/scratch/x86sim
# A Mac, for comparison with Apple's simulator only (tests/apps/sdkcov/ref-mac.sh, webcov --ref-mac; ssh, BatchMode),
# e.g. user@my-mac.local. Nothing is built there: everything builds on Linux (docs/LINUX-BUILD.md).
X86SIM_MAC=
# FoundationModels (Apple's on-device language model) on iOS and visionOS devices, run on a Mac with Apple Intelligence
# as Apple's simulators do (docs/DEVELOPMENT.md): 1 = $X86SIM_MAC, or user@host (ssh, BatchMode). Empty or 0: off, the
# model reports modelNotReady. Takes effect at the next boot of a device.
#X86SIM_FM_RELAY=

# GPU (Metal stand-in on Vulkan): X86SIM_METAL=all (default: every process), 0 (software rendering) or a
# comma-separated process list. Which GPU: each device has its own setting (x86sim gpu [PHONE] set auto|cpu|NAME|ID,
# the launcher's Advanced > GPU); auto takes a discrete GPU first. For boot-a64.sh run by hand: MTLVK_DEVICE_ID=<id>
# (from `mtlvk --list-gpus`), cpu or auto (docs/METAL.md). The Vulkan loader / Mesa variables still apply, e.g.
# VK_ICD_FILENAMES=/usr/share/vulkan/icd.d/nvidia_icd.json or MESA_VK_DEVICE_SELECT=1002:73bf
#X86SIM_METAL=all
#MTLVK_DEVICE_ID=auto
#VK_ICD_FILENAMES=
#MESA_VK_DEVICE_SELECT=
# mtlvk binary (make mtlvk)
#X86SIM_MTLVK=$X86SIM_ROOT/linux/mtlvk/target/release/mtlvk


# Disk guard: a phone runs in a systemd user scope capped at X86SIM_IO_MAX bytes/s of reads and writes (default 40M;
# 0 = no scope) and X86SIM_IO_IOPS reads/s (default 4000), with low IO/CPU weight (X86SIM_CPU_WEIGHT, default 40).
# Without a systemd user manager there is no scope: the caps (and X86SIM_MEM_MAX) are not applied.
#X86SIM_IO_MAX=40M
